<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Permits plain HTTP, which Android has blocked by default since API 28. Two things in this app need it,
  and they are not equally harmless.

  The first is GET http://<rendezvous>:21114/key, which is plain HTTP by design; the desktop's
  NetworkSettingsViewModel.FetchKeyFromServerAsync fetches the same URL the same way. What it returns is a
  public key: intercepting it reveals nothing, and substituting it only lets an attacker impersonate hosts
  on a server they would have to control anyway.

  The second is the portal API, and that one carries a device token in an Authorization header. Over plain
  HTTP that token is readable by anything on the path, and it is enough to read and rewrite the account's
  saved list of computers. The portal's address is typed by the user, so this policy does not choose plain
  HTTP for them; it only declines to forbid it, because a portal on a private network may have no
  certificate to offer. https://deskpair.app, the one this app defaults to, is HTTPS.

  Neither address can be named in a domain-config here, because both are whatever the user types.

  None of this governs the session itself. A DeskPair session is a raw TCP socket carrying its own
  ECDH + AES-GCM handshake, not HTTPS, so the cleartext policy never applied to it either way.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
